// Lesson 1.6a — Animation States: IsWalking, WalkDir, Attack
// Documentation-first: Animator API, SpriteRenderer.flipX, state machine separation.
// Non-exact example (SecurityBot) + Student specification for PlayerController.cs.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.6a — Animation States: IsWalking, WalkDir, Attack</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your player moves through space, but it does not <em>look</em> alive yet. Today, you connect code
    to Unity's Animator state machine: reading parameters, driving directional clips, flipping sprites,
    and understanding why code sets state while the engine plays frames.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        In professional game engines, scripts rarely swap individual sprite images directly.
        Instead, the visual engine uses an <strong>Animator Controller</strong> — a visual finite state machine.
        Your C# script communicates with the Animator by setting <strong>parameters</strong>:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Engine API</th><th>Signature / Type</th><th>Description &amp; Behavior</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>animator.SetBool()</code></td>
            <td><code>void SetBool(string name, bool value)</code></td>
            <td>Sets a boolean condition parameter on the Animator Controller. Used for persistent continuous states (e.g. idle vs walking).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>animator.SetInteger()</code></td>
            <td><code>void SetInteger(string name, int value)</code></td>
            <td>Sets an integer parameter. Used for discrete multi-state selection (e.g. direction: 0 = up, 1 = side, 2 = down).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>animator.SetTrigger()</code></td>
            <td><code>void SetTrigger(string name)</code></td>
            <td>Fires a momentary trigger parameter that resets automatically once consumed by a transition. Used for one-shot actions like attacks or jumps.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>spriteRenderer.flipX</code></td>
            <td><code>bool</code> (read/write)</td>
            <td>Horizontally mirrors the sprite texture across its vertical axis. Allows a single sideways walk animation to serve for both left and right.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Mathf.Abs()</code></td>
            <td><code>float Mathf.Abs(float f)</code></td>
            <td>Returns the absolute value of a number (stripping negative signs). Essential for comparing speed magnitudes regardless of direction.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Separation of Concerns</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why does the C# script set abstract parameters like <code>"IsWalking"</code> or <code>"WalkDir"</code> instead of telling the engine "play frame 4 of the walk sprite sheet"?</li>
            <li>What would happen to your code if an artist decided to replace a 4-frame walk animation with an 8-frame walk animation? If your code only sets parameters, does any C# have to change?</li>
            <li>What is the technical difference between setting a boolean with <code>SetBool</code> versus firing an action with <code>SetTrigger</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Notice how each visual function and parameter type works in isolation across completely different characters:
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — Setting a Boolean State on a Scuba Diver:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Drives a continuous state transition (e.g. idle on boat vs swimming underwater)
animator.SetBool("IsSwimming", waterDepth &gt; 0f);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — Setting an Integer Discrete State on a Warrior:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Selects an equipment stance: 0 = Unarmed, 1 = Sword/Shield, 2 = Greatsword
animator.SetInteger("WeaponStance", 2);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — Firing a One-Shot Action Trigger on a Wizard:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Fires a momentary action clip that plays once and returns to the default state
if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space))
{
    animator.SetTrigger("CastSpell");
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — Flipping a 2D Sprite on a Flying Falcon:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Horizontally mirrors the sprite texture based on whether flight velocity is negative
spriteRenderer.flipX = horizontalVelocity &lt; 0f;</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — Stripping Negative Signs with Absolute Value:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Mathf.Abs turns -4.5 into +4.5, making it easy to compare magnitudes regardless of sign
if (Mathf.Abs(steerX) &gt;= Mathf.Abs(steerY))
{
    // Horizontal magnitude is stronger than vertical
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If <code>steerX</code> is <code>-0.8</code> and <code>steerY</code> is <code>0.3</code>, what does <code>Mathf.Abs(steerX) &gt;= Mathf.Abs(steerY)</code> evaluate to?</li>
            <li>Why is <code>SetTrigger</code> preferred over <code>SetBool</code> for an attack swing or spell cast?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — Read the Animator Controller in Unity.</strong>
        Select the Player in the Overworld scene, then open <strong>Window → Animation → Animator</strong>.
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>Click the <strong>Parameters</strong> tab in the top-left of the Animator window. Notice the three parameters already configured: <code>IsWalking</code> (Bool), <code>WalkDir</code> (Int), and <code>Attack</code> (Trigger).</li>
        <li>Click the transition arrow pointing from the side-walking state to the idle state. In the Inspector, read its condition: <code>IsWalking == false</code>.</li>
        <li>Click the arrows between up, side, and down: notice they evaluate <code>WalkDir equals 0</code> (up), <code>1</code> (side), and <code>2</code> (down).</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>PlayerController.cs</code>:</strong>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <strong>Component References:</strong>
                In <code>PlayerController.cs</code>, declare private fields for <code>Animator anim</code> and <code>SpriteRenderer sprite</code>.
                In <code>Start()</code>, initialize both using <code>GetComponentInChildren&lt;Animator&gt;()</code> and <code>GetComponentInChildren&lt;SpriteRenderer&gt;()</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Create <code>UpdateAnimation()</code> Method:</strong>
                Declare a private helper method named <code>UpdateAnimation()</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Sub-Problem 1 (Evaluate Walking State):</strong>
                Calculate a boolean <code>isMoving</code> that is true if either <code>xDirection != 0</code> or <code>yDirection != 0</code>.
                Call <code>anim.SetBool("IsWalking", isMoving);</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Sub-Problem 2 (Evaluate Direction &amp; Flip):</strong>
                If <code>isMoving</code> is true:
                <ul>
                    <li>Use <code>Mathf.Abs()</code> to compare <code>xDirection</code> and <code>yDirection</code>.</li>
                    <li>If horizontal movement is greater than or equal to vertical movement: set integer parameter <code>"WalkDir"</code> to <code>1</code>, and set <code>sprite.flipX = xDirection &gt; 0;</code> (or adjust based on your sprite's default texture facing).</li>
                    <li>Otherwise (vertical dominates): if <code>yDirection &gt; 0</code>, set <code>"WalkDir"</code> to <code>0</code> (up) and <code>sprite.flipX = true;</code>; if <code>yDirection &lt; 0</code>, set <code>"WalkDir"</code> to <code>2</code> (down) and <code>sprite.flipX = false;</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Sub-Problem 3 (Attack Trigger):</strong>
                Check if the left mouse button was clicked using <code>Input.GetMouseButtonDown(0)</code>. If clicked, call <code>anim.SetTrigger("Attack");</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Call from Update:</strong>
                Call <code>UpdateAnimation();</code> at the very bottom of your <code>Update()</code> loop.
            </li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save <code>PlayerController.cs</code> and return to Unity. Press Play.
        Walk in all four directions. Your character should now smoothly transition between idle, walking sideways, walking up, and walking down, with proper left/right sprite flipping!
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Diagnosing Animation Faults</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if you misspell <code>"IsWalking"</code> as <code>"isWalking"</code> (lowercase i) in your C# code? What does the Unity Console report?</li>
            <li>Temporarily comment out the line setting <code>sprite.flipX</code>. Which direction can your player never face?</li>
            <li>What happens if you click the mouse rapidly? Does the attack animation trigger cleanly and return to walking/idle?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Exploring State Transitions</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Open the Animator window while the game is running in Play mode. Walk around. Notice the blue progress bar on each animation state indicating playback time, and observe the transitions highlighting in real time!</li>
            <li>The player prefab includes animation clips for holding tools/weapons (e.g. shovel, axe). In the Animator window, find where the alternate weapon states are located. What conditions govern their transitions?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Open the Animator Controller window and inspect states, arrows, and parameter conditions</li>
            <li>Explain the distinct purposes of <code>SetBool</code>, <code>SetInteger</code>, and <code>SetTrigger</code></li>
            <li>Explain the software architecture split: code sets state parameters, the Animator Controller plays visual clips</li>
            <li>Use <code>Mathf.Abs()</code> to resolve dominant directional input</li>
            <li>Commit your working animation controller integration to GitHub</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

